2026-10-18|Minimum Edges from Vertex 1 to Vertex N

Question:
Given a directed graph with vertices numbered from 1 to n, an edge exists from vertex i to vertex j if and only if:
- j = i + 1
- j = 3 * i

Find the minimum number of edges in a path from vertex 1 to vertex n.

Approach:

1. Create a queue and insert vertex 1. Set its distance to 0 because no edges are needed to reach the starting vertex.
2. Maintain a distance array initialized to -1. A value of -1 means the vertex has not been visited.
3. Remove the front vertex i from the queue.
4. Generate its possible next vertices:
   - i + 1, if i + 1 <= n.
   - 3 * i, if 3 * i <= n.
5. For each unvisited next vertex, set its distance to distance[i] + 1 and add it to the queue.
6. Continue until vertex n is reached. Its distance is the minimum number of edges required.
7. If n = 1, the answer is 0 because the starting and destination vertices are the same.

Solution:
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <queue>
using namespace std;

int minEdges(int n) {
    vector<int> dist(n + 1, -1);
    queue<int> q;

    q.push(1);
    dist[1] = 0;

    while (!q.empty()) {
        int i = q.front();
        q.pop();

        if (i == n) {
            return dist[i];
        }

        int next1 = i + 1;
        if (next1 <= n && dist[next1] == -1) {
            dist[next1] = dist[i] + 1;
            q.push(next1);
        }

        int next2 = 3 * i;
        if (next2 <= n && dist[next2] == -1) {
            dist[next2] = dist[i] + 1;
            q.push(next2);
        }
    }

    return -1;
}

int main() {
    int n;
    cin >> n;

    cout << minEdges(n) << '\n';

    return 0;
}
```

Time Complexity: O(n), because each vertex is visited at most once and has at most two outgoing edges.

Space Complexity: O(n), for the distance array and BFS queue.
